## Setup

Supported hosts: macOS and Linux. Application scripts use portable POSIX `sh`.
Bash 4 or newer is required by the coverage tooling; on macOS put Homebrew's
`bin` directory before `/bin` in `PATH` rather than using Apple's Bash 3.2.

On macOS, install prerequisites with Homebrew:

```sh
brew install just bash dash ksh zsh shellcheck shfmt checkbashisms codespell semgrep bats-core shellspec kcov
export PATH="$(brew --prefix)/bin:$PATH"
```

On Linux, install `just`, Bash 4+, dash, ksh, zsh, BusyBox, ShellCheck, shfmt,
checkbashisms (from Debian's `devscripts` package), codespell, Semgrep,
Bats-core, ShellSpec, and Kcov 38+. See the upstream installation links below.
The template repository includes `docker/Dockerfile.linux-ci` with a complete
Linux toolchain installation and a Linux baseline/violation test recipe.

Then run:

```sh
just init
just run
just ci
```

`init` checks prerequisites, prepares report directories, makes project scripts
executable (including extensionless scripts identified by their shebang), and
activates the generated pre-commit hook. Missing prerequisites fail immediately;
checks never silently disappear because a tool or shell is unavailable.

## Project layout

```text
src/main.sh             Application entry point
scripts/               Validation and test helpers
test/test-main.sh      Plain executable tests
test/bats/main.bats    Bats integration tests
spec/main_spec.sh      ShellSpec behavior and coverage tests
config/semgrep/        Local policy rules
data/input/           Input data
data/output/          Output data
reports/              Generated reports
justfile              Development and CI commands
AGENTS.md             Coding conventions
CLAUDE.md             Link to AGENTS.md
```

## Validation

| Target | Tool and purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `code-format` | shfmt; explicitly apply formatting |
| `code-style` | shfmt; reject formatting differences without changing files |
| `code-shellcheck` | ShellCheck; quoting, expansion, command and shell correctness |
| `code-syntax` | Native parsers; validate each script under its supported shells |
| `code-portability` | checkbashisms; reject non-POSIX constructs in portable scripts |
| `code-semgrep` | Semgrep; local security and coding policies |
| `code-spell` | codespell; check code and documentation spelling |
| `test` | Execute every plain `test*` shell script recursively; nonzero means failure |
| `test-matrix` | Run plain tests under each supported interpreter |
| `test-spec` | ShellSpec behavior tests under each supported interpreter |
| `test-bats` | Bats CLI integration tests |
| `test-coverage` | ShellSpec with Kcov; enforce {{coverage_threshold}}% source line coverage using Bash |
| `ci` | All validation gates, failing immediately on error |
| `ci-quiet` | The exact same CI pipeline, showing captured output when a check fails |

Formatting changes are deliberate: run `just code-format` locally. CI only
checks formatting. Both CI modes include all check and test tools above.

The interpreter matrix is `sh`, `bash`, `dash`, `ksh`, and `zsh` on macOS;
Linux adds `busybox ash`. Product scripts are portable by default. A Bash
shebang explicitly selects Bash-specific syntax checks; changing a shebang does
not make a script portable. Run application code through `TEST_SHELL` in plain
tests or `When run script` in ShellSpec tests to exercise the selected interpreter.
Interpreter tests do not emulate another operating system's external utilities:
run CI on both macOS and Linux to verify BSD/GNU command behavior.

## Adding tests

Add a shell script whose filename starts with `test` under `test/`, normally
`test/test-feature.sh`. Other extensions and extensionless shell scripts are
also discovered by their shebang; `.bats` files run in `test-bats`. Include a
supported shell shebang and `set -eu`, assert
the expected behavior, and return a nonzero status on failure. Run `just init`
after adding scripts to set execution permissions. The runner rejects an empty
test suite and reports the failing filename. It preserves filenames containing
spaces and newlines and does not execute unrelated files as shell scripts.

For example:

```sh
#!/bin/sh
set -eu
output=$(sh src/main.sh 'Shell World')
[ "$output" = 'Hello, Shell World!' ]
```

Use `test/test-main.sh` as the example for matrix-aware tests. Add `.bats` files
under `test/bats/` for Bats and `_spec.sh` files under `spec/` for ShellSpec.
Test missing/empty input, whitespace and special characters, subprocess failure,
and cleanup behavior as features are added.

## Tool references

- [ShellCheck](https://github.com/koalaman/shellcheck#installing)
- [shfmt](https://github.com/mvdan/sh)
- [checkbashisms](https://manpages.debian.org/testing/devscripts/checkbashisms.1.en.html)
- [Semgrep](https://semgrep.dev/docs/getting-started/)
- [codespell](https://github.com/codespell-project/codespell)
- [Bats-core](https://github.com/bats-core/bats-core)
- [ShellSpec](https://github.com/shellspec/shellspec)
- [Kcov](https://github.com/SimonKagstrom/kcov)

Semgrep complements ShellCheck with repository-specific policy checks. CodeQL
does not support shell code analysis, and execution wrappers such as
`semantic-wrap` are not static analysis tools, so neither is a CI gate here.
Coverage describes executed source lines, not type safety or proof of correctness.
The coverage helper uses Bash for its macOS file-descriptor limit adjustment.
It starts a fresh Kcov collection on every run, so previous results cannot mask
a coverage regression. `just destroy` removes generated reports.
